    `Isaiah 43:2 does not say if you pass through the waters. It says when. The difficulty is assumed. The crisis is not presented as a possible exception to the normal Christian life — it is presented as the normal Christian life, the terrain through which the person who walks with God will inevitably pass. Waters. Rivers. Fire. Flames. The imagery is comprehensive and it is honest. What God promises is not the prevention of the difficulty. He promises His presence inside it. I will be with you. They will not sweep over you. You will not be burned. Joseph is the clearest extended portrait in Scripture of what it looks like to trust God through the most difficult time. Betrayed by his brothers, sold into slavery, falsely accused by Potiphar's wife, imprisoned for years, forgotten by the man he helped. Every time the story appears to be resolving, it gets worse. And at every point, the same phrase surfaces: the Lord was with Joseph. Not above the difficulty, looking down at it. With Joseph. Inside the pit. Inside the prison. Inside the years of silence. The presence of God in Joseph's story did not make the circumstances comfortable. It made them survivable. And it made them purposeful — because the pit and the prison were not obstacles to the plan. They were the route through which the plan arrived. Psalm 46 was written from a place of genuine upheaval — the earth giving way, mountains falling, waters roaring. This is not metaphorical comfort offered from a position of safety. It is the testimony of people in the middle of the shaking who have found something that does not shake. God is our refuge and strength, an ever-present help in trouble. The word translated ever-present in Hebrew is the word for found — He is found to be a very present help. He is not theoretically available. He is found — discovered, experienced, encountered — precisely in the trouble. The place of the most intense difficulty is the place where the presence of God becomes most tangibly real, because it is the place where every other source of strength has been exhausted and only He remains. Romans 8:28 is the theological anchor for every difficult season: all things work together for good for those who love God and are called according to His purpose. Not some things. Not the comfortable things. All things. Including the betrayal, the false accusation, the imprisonment, the years of silence. The good that God is working toward is not always the good we were asking for. Joseph did not ask to spend two years in prison after correctly interpreting the cupbearer's dream. But the two years in prison produced the man who could stand before Pharaoh. The difficulty was the preparation.`
