    `The Holy Spirit is the most neglected person of the Trinity in most evangelical Christianity. The Father is understood — God above, creator, sovereign, judge. The Son is understood — Jesus, historical, incarnate, crucified, risen. But the Spirit remains vague for most believers — a force, an influence, an atmosphere, a feeling in worship, a theological category that is acknowledged but not inhabited. This vagueness is not merely an intellectual problem. It is a practical one. Because the Spirit is not a force. He is a person. And the difference between treating Him as a force and knowing Him as a person is the difference between having power available in theory and accessing it in daily reality. Jesus used a specific word for the Spirit in John 14 — Paraclete. It is one of the richest words in the Greek New Testament. It means advocate, counsellor, helper, comforter, one called alongside. It is the word used in legal contexts for the person who stands beside the accused and argues their case. It is the word used for the experienced guide who walks beside the traveller through unfamiliar terrain. Jesus says the Father will give you another Paraclete — another of the same kind as Jesus Himself. The Spirit who was sent to be with the disciples was not a lesser substitute for Jesus's presence. He was the same quality of personal, present, actively engaged companionship — now available not just to those who were physically near Jesus, but to every believer everywhere, forever. Romans 8:26–27 gives us the most intimate portrait of the Spirit's work in the believer: He helps us in our weakness. We do not know what we ought to pray for, but the Spirit Himself intercedes for us through wordless groans. The Spirit does not merely assist us in prayer. He prays inside us — taking the inarticulate, unformed, wordless depths of our need and translating them into intercession that is perfectly aligned with the will of God. This is not a peripheral spiritual gift. This is the daily reality available to every believer who has received the Spirit. In the moments when you do not have words for what you are carrying — the Spirit has words. In the moments when you do not know what to ask for — the Spirit knows. In the moments when your weakness is most visible and most felt — the Spirit is most actively at work inside you. He also convicts — not to condemn, but to bring to light what needs to change so that change becomes possible. He guides — into truth that you could not find by intellectual effort alone. He empowers — giving capacity for witness, service, and holiness that exceeds what natural ability can produce. He comforts — the same root word as Paraclete, the presence that settles the unsettled soul. He sanctifies — the ongoing work of making the believer more like Christ, not by external pressure but by internal transformation. This is the person who lives inside you. This is who you have access to. This is the one Jesus said it was better for the disciples that He go away so that the Spirit could come. Not a downgrade. An upgrade. The same Jesus, now inside rather than alongside, available to every believer rather than limited to one body in one location.`
